{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448685-how-would-you-handle-a-story-set-in-human-and-kindred-prehistory","id":1448685,"title":"How would you handle a story set in human (and kindred) prehistory?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-16T07:29:15-07:00","id":1448685,"title":"How would you handle a story set in human (and kindred) prehistory?","content":"Admittedly, this wrecks the Masquerade part of the game, but I've had the idea circling in my head for a while, now. I really like the mental imagery of early humans gathered together in the dark, knowing that beyond the light of their campfire the dead walk the earth preying on the living, and other (worse) things besides. There's no civilisation as their descendants will know it, no one else to protect them yet. All they have to defend themselves is their family, their wits and hunting tools (and, if you're very lucky, a benevolent mage in the family or a spirit or something.) It could go in a very cosmic horror type direction.<br /> And then there are vampires, having to find their way in a very rough and empty world. Even vampires don't have a society to turn to yet, though they might start trying to build one. Would they have to hide from their old life, or would their group hesitantly tolerate them, since they might be useful against enemies and the other monsters?<br /> <br /> And how much of what is known of vampire's origins is myth, and what is fact?<br /> <br /> I'm thinking if I could somehow use this for a game I'd have Caine and Lilith being myths with some historical roots, with the first generation being plural. The first city is maybe a myth (or is it?), but it would be interesting to play with the themes of the first undead playing god and building attempts at early civilisations around them. I also have this image in my head of there being vampires <i>before</i> the first generation, which were decidedly<i> not </i>human. Possibly the first vampires are &quot;embraced&quot; by dying in certain circumstances or due to crimes committed in life that attracted the thing to their dying body. Mass murderers, cannibals, etc. (Which leads into the question of &quot;what happened to the world that this stopped happening? Where did those things <i>go</i>? What if they come back?&quot;<br /> (That'd be a plot twist. The Sabbat were expecting the return of the third generation, Gehenna hits and they get something even older and much worse. Maybe all kindred are wiped out all the way up to the highest generations and the cycle starts up again.)<br /> I don't see murder as being a new invention at this stage, since killing each other was invented a long time before humans turned up. But I could see the essence of vampirism being born from the first ever occurrence of premeditated killing of a sentient being waaaaay back as a thing that could've happened. (If that's even possible to happen)<br /> <br /> Of course, the Book of Nod could be right, too. I don't know how well playing Jyhad in the First or Second City would go...<br /> <br /> The plus of using prehistory is that it's vague, and we don't have 100% concrete ideas of what life was like. The con of using prehistory is that it's vague, and we don't have 100% concrete ideas of what life was like.<br /> <br /> Should the clans be included without the original founders, making the origins and the Antediluvians a mystery? Or should everybody be Caitiff, with the clan distinctions having yet to evolve? Or are the legends of the Antediluvians very much real?<br /> <br /> I'm also not clear on what the shapeshifters were up to at this point, but in fairness I'm already potentially dismissing Vampire lore for this, what's the harm in mangling Werewolf history whilst I\u2019m at it? Nobody's propaganda matches up in this setting anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 05-16-2021, 11:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T07:58:55-07:00","id":1448690,"title":"","content":"It depends if you want to pysle out your players and project a tone of mystery make everyone Cartriffs and the Antediluvian, a myth hurriedly spoke about","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T09:02:32-07:00","id":1448695,"title":"","content":"&quot;Where&quot; and &quot;when&quot; will be important terms here. Pre-history covers a large portion of human. 4000 BCE Mesopotamia will be different than 7000 BCE Doggerland.<br /> <br /> Going on that, do some research into the place and time you want to set the game.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T09:35:03-07:00","id":1448697,"title":"","content":"The thing is, the world of folklore is sooo different from the world of science that you basically have to start by deciding which is true. Is the WOD actually a folklore world, and the scientific theories about how it began just a bunch of quakery vampires supported for the sake of the masquerade? If so, then a whole bunch of bards have done your work for you. Just look up various religious stories and base your campaign around them being true. Your players become basically biblical Xena Warrior Princesses in this scenario.<br /> <br /> If you decide that science is mostly right, then you have a harder job to do. The story of Caine can't possibly be true, as humans were killing each other long before they were even completely human. So in this scenario Caine has to be something else, maybe something Lovecraftian, and the stories about him a massive distortion of that Lovecraftian horror.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T09:55:58-07:00","id":1448700,"title":"","content":"I agree with others said that as an ST you have to decide how you want to handle \u2018The Truth\u2019, make sure your players are on board, and then build from there.<br /> <br /> The five big avenues I see are Agnostic Mysticism where you go with a heavily magical and folkloric background but leave the question of the Abrahamic G-d ambiguous (Requiem style), Noddism style where the Book of Nod/Revelations of the Dark Mother are (mostly) accurate, Eldritch where it\u2019s a mystery where these monsters come from and perhaps they simply crawl from the pits of cosmic darkness, Scientific where the scientific consensus is pretty much correct but also vampires, and Multi-Resonance Layered Reality where after The Fall you have consensus shaping the truth of the situation for these vampires and mortals.<br /> <br /> The background you mention is also pretty similar to how Requiem handles much of its metaplot so you might get some inspiration from looking through the old VtR clan books and stuff like Dark Ages and Blood &amp; Smoke. <br /> <br /> For the avenue you\u2019re discussing I also think you could benefit from having a bunch of different manifestations of archetypical Dark Father &amp; Dark Mother figures like what we get from the Book of Nod, Laibon, etc. with many similar yet distinct stories for the origins of post-DF vampires.<br /> <br /> I really think you just need to kind of feel things out and talk with your table and see what sounds like the most fun for everyone and then roll out from there. Wish ya the best, sounds like a fun game coming up!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T10:19:57-07:00","id":1448705,"title":"","content":"Making the characters and their ghouls the beings that inspired myths of gods and demons might be kind of interesting...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25011-taggie","name":"Taggie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1047"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T11:21:06-07:00","id":1448708,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Taggie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448685-how-would-you-handle-a-story-set-in-human-and-kindred-prehistory?p=1448705#post1448705\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Making the characters and their ghouls the beings that inspired myths of gods and demons might be kind of interesting...</div> </div> </div> </div>That would also be a great thing to surprise or reward players with when they play in games set after the flood. Also plots where archaeological evidence of their characters turns up in the modern day and/or triggers an antediluvian panic.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Does anyone know if there are dates given for events in the book of nod?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 05-16-2021, 08:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T14:12:33-07:00","id":1448716,"title":"","content":"This is how I handle pre-historical times in my chronicles. For my case, it's not to run any games in that era. It's just to help me work out certain facts so I know how to portray certain NPCs, figure out timelines, or determine the lore of my setting.<br /> <br /> The end of the Pleistocene marks the boundary between the antedeluvial world and this one around 12,000 BC to 10,000 BC. Real science can't provide real facts or evidence of that world because too much depends on spiritual reality, things that were shifted to the other side of the Gauntlet, etc. Evidence about the antedeluvial world are only found in fringe pseudo-science, and even that is divided between different pseudo-scientific theories. There are Biblical scholars approaching things from that perspective. Occultists from the perspective of Atlantis, Mu, Hyperborea, or other crackpot theories. Various ancient alien astronaut theories. Pulp concepts like Robert E. Howard's Thurian and Hyborian Ages and Cataclysm. And more restrained theories like the work of Graham Hancock about Ice Age Civilizations. Prior to modern scholars, many civilizations had their own ideas as well like the Flood of Deucalion, Sumerian myths, etc. It's not entirely clear what the Deluge was. The term is just a placeholder for some kind of catastrophic event. This time is essentially pre-WoD. It will always be a complete mystery.<br /> <br /> After the Deluge is when &quot;real&quot; history begins. Right after this time is when I have the Changing Breeds be created. The Antedeluvian Vampire Clan Founders survived the Deluge, but were not immediately active afterwards. They probably rose from their watery tombs around 8000 BC and built the second city, best known to humans as Babel. They were recognized as the Apkallu in Sumeria/Akkadia/Babylonian myth - the sages who taught men the arts of civilization. Ventrue was probably Oannes himself. It's possible that the Sumerian language itself (a language isolate) was a vestige of the antedeluvian world. That city is now buried under tons of silt and water in the Persian Gulf, but at the time was dry land. Not every antedeluvian stayed at Babel. Ravnos left to explore east and eventually found the Indus Valley. Gangrel moved on immediately. Nosferatu and Tzimisce did not stay long either. <br /> <br /> The antedeluvians sent themselves up as god-kings, and after their Babel project collapsed, they dispersed into separate city states in Mesopotamia. The antedeluvians themselves had vast experience and ready power from before the Flood, so they had no reason to fear the Lupines and other Changing Breeds that occupied the rest of the world. But any new childer they created, despite their potent blood, were easy prey until they had accrued sufficient experience. The Lupines could not destroy the cities of the vampire god-kings themselves, protected as they were by the antedeluvians. But the Lupines did establish the Impergium to prevent other cities from being established so that the younger vampires could not find places to hide and spread.<br /> <br /> The antedeluvian themselves entered torpor around 6000 BC after several thousand years of rule, although some were supposed to have been destroyed by their childer (or others) shortly before or at this time. It's entirely possible that the destruction, of say Ventrue, is what prompted the rest to seek the safety of torpor. By that time, their favored childer were experienced and powerful enough that they were very powerful and could defend their city states from Lupines and other enemies. There was no 5th generation before this as the antedeluvians simply did not allow their childer to create any of their own, and they had Disciplines and abilities to do so. The various members of the 4th generation could now do whatever they wanted, including sire children. Some of the antedeluvians would rise up now and again for the next several thousand years to move, create new childer, or do something else in regards to the Jyhad, but essentially 6000 BC is when they stopped being active for any long term. Typhon moved to Egypt around this time, and embraced new childer who would form the basis of the Set/Osiris myth there.<br /> <br /> The next three thousand years is the rest of pre-history. Vampires for the most part stayed within Mesopotamia as the only urban areas, and they all ruled as divine god kings. Some of the more powerful childer risked leaving the area and survived the Lupines to eventually establish lineages in other lands. It wasn't until the Lupines ended the Impergium that urbanization spread to other lands, and vampires began infesting the rest of the world. Of course, some vampires descended from Gangrel or Nosferatu were already there, but they were relatively few.<br /> <br /> Most Stone Age peoples did not need to huddle in the dark before their fires and fear the night because of vampires. That was because Lupines were everywhere and could slaughter most vampires. What Stone Age peoples feared for the most part was the anger of their own protectors as well as malevolent spirits. It would be some time before vampires would be able to rule the night outside the Mesopotamian city states. The conditions were not yet right for most vampires to prosper there. Those that could survive were those so ancient and powerful, they would be remembered as gods, not monsters.<br /> <br /> That's what I use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T14:44:46-07:00","id":1448718,"title":"","content":"The world is more magical, mages are more powerful, mystical beasts prowl around, and the generations are incredibly low.<br /> <br /> As for how to run it, basically its a low powered Abyssal Exalted game with a night time activity requirement.<br /> <br /> The main thing to get right is the human element. At best you will have city states with limited populations and a few wandering tribes. In essence you have to be some what careful about slaughtering your own food supply. Like wise the total cainite population in the area will be limited, like one clan controls a city state with a few odd ball guests in residence, but no more than a couple dozen kindred per area.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T15:13:22-07:00","id":1448719,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448685-how-would-you-handle-a-story-set-in-human-and-kindred-prehistory?p=1448716#post1448716\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most Stone Age peoples did not need to huddle in the dark before their fires and fear the night because of vampires. That was because Lupines were everywhere and could slaughter most vampires. What Stone Age peoples feared for the most part was the anger of their own protectors as well as malevolent spirits. It would be some time before vampires would be able to rule the night outside the Mesopotamian city states. The conditions were not yet right for most vampires to prosper there. Those that could survive were those so ancient and powerful, they would be remembered as gods, not monsters.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had been wondering about kinfolk, fera and tribes, but WtA is the setting I'm least familiar with so I wasn't sure what to do. I was just pretty sure they couldn't have been that commonly encountered in the earliest nights, or else vampires would be extremely extinct. Well, I'll see about getting a copy of Shattered Dreams, wiki says that covers the prehistory.<br /> <br /> If a vampire could manage to sire one of them (the humans), waking up dead and later, by some insanely unlikely miracle, survivng their clan protectors chasing them off would certainly be one hell of a welcome to undeath for a new fledgling.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448685-how-would-you-handle-a-story-set-in-human-and-kindred-prehistory?p=1448718#post1448718\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The world is more magical, mages are more powerful, mystical beasts prowl around, and the generations are incredibly low.<br /> <br /> As for how to run it, basically its a low powered Abyssal Exalted game with a night time activity requirement.<br /> <br /> The main thing to get right is the human element. At best you will have city states with limited populations and a few wandering tribes. In essence you have to be some what careful about slaughtering your own food supply. Like wise the total cainite population in the area will be limited, like one clan controls a city state with a few odd ball guests in residence, but no more than a couple dozen kindred per area.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not familiar with Exalted, but the low vampire population was exactly the theme I had in mind (also the terrifying levels of magic and primordial things that run on totally alien rules who no longer exist except in dreams and nightmares). Assuming a setting with multiple of the first generation, or that some ancient vampires did gangrel sire them and leave them style embraces to make sure their companions weren't weaklings, these guys could've wandered around totally alone for centuries without meetng another of their kind, with no idea what the hell's happened to them. Most probably devolved into wights from fear, hunger and loneliness and got put down by the bigger monsters or fell into torpor (one of those waking up in the modern day would be an interesting event). A lot probably commited suicide, sooner or later. How long would it take before they work out that the embrace even exists, let alone how it works? It's not exactly intuitive without a mentor or vampire pop culture to make it common knowledge.<br /> <br /> Actually at this stage in the thought process, I find the idea that there were multiple of the first generation and only one of them managed to live long enough to work out how vampirism worked and be remembered makes a lot of sense.<br /> <br /> For a group of players, it's a small group who have possibly never seen another vampire before realising that there are other things like them out there for the first time, after being alone for so long they can't count the years (if years are even a concept at this point, really).<br /> In their backstories they might have frenzied and slaughtered their own tribe, or been driven out immediately, abandoned the first day they went into torpor, or abandoned later when their closest loved ones died and the newer generation didn't want to tolerate the monster hanging around leeching off their blood.<br /> <br /> And fledglings in the modern nights think they have it hard(!)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 05-16-2021, 06:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T15:43:19-07:00","id":1448721,"title":"","content":"When I wrote &quot;research&quot; I meant broadly and for the aesthetics. By 4000 BCE in Mesopotamia long term settlements and communities existed, as did farming, around the rivers across the plans which were still grasslands. 7000 BCE in Doggerland would have seen nomadic groups of hunter gatherers moving about seasonally across a varied (but cooler and wetter) region. There is a world of difference between stone-age hunter gathers in Northern Europe and settled agricultural people in the Middle East 3K years later.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T16:20:19-07:00","id":1448723,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rhywbeth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448685-how-would-you-handle-a-story-set-in-human-and-kindred-prehistory?p=1448719#post1448719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not familiar with Exalted, but the low vampire population was exactly the theme I had in mind (also the terrifying levels of magic and primordial things that run on totally alien rules who no longer exist except in dreams and nightmares). Assuming a setting with multiple of the first generation, or that some ancient vampires did gangrel sire them and leave them style embraces to make sure their companions weren't weaklings, these guys could've wandered around totally alone for centuries without meetng another of their kind, with no idea what the hell's happened to them. Most probably devolved into wights from fear, hunger and loneliness and got put down by the bigger monsters or fell into torpor (one of those waking up in the modern day would be an interesting event). A lot probably commited suicide, sooner or later. How long would it take before they work out that the embrace even exists, let alone how it works? It's not exactly intuitive without a mentor or vampire pop culture to make it common knowledge.</div> </div> </div> </div>In its first edition, Exalted was pitched as the true prehistory setting to the World of Darkness. Each exalt type lines up with a game line of the time with Abyssals being the cainite analog. Power wise Caine would have been a low powered abyssal or an abyssal half-caste. Exalted is for when you want to do things like punch a continent sized enemy so hard that the laws of reality change as a result. <br /> <br /> Personally I don't see most vampires wandering all that far from their either their birth lands or their main food supply. Of course the gangrel tend to wander and some characters are just the adventurous types, but when you consider high powered shifters and mythical beasts wandering around the world, staying in your capital city just makes more sense. Imagine if the titular character in the Lucifer tv series just stayed in LA for centuries. Every night is a party, the mortals are conditioned to supply you with blood and companionship, all the while you take the lions share of the wealth. In turn you are the unliving repository for all knowledge in the city, the law giver for the city, and the healer of serious wounds when you bestow your blood.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T17:09:11-07:00","id":1448727,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448685-how-would-you-handle-a-story-set-in-human-and-kindred-prehistory?p=1448723#post1448723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I don't see most vampires wandering all that far from their either their birth lands or their main food supply. Of course the gangrel tend to wander and some characters are just the adventurous types, but when you consider high powered shifters and mythical beasts wandering around the world, staying in your capital city just makes more sense. Imagine if the titular character in the Lucifer tv series just stayed in LA for centuries. Every night is a party, the mortals are conditioned to supply you with blood and companionship, all the while you take the lions share of the wealth. In turn you are the unliving repository for all knowledge in the city, the law giver for the city, and the healer of serious wounds when you bestow your blood.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I had Caine's &quot;cursed to wander the earth alone&quot; thing at the forefront of my brain (was that part of the curse, or am I imagining things?)<br /> <br /> Taking over a city is the goal for a vampire who wants to survive, provided that there is a city around to rule (and that they've been invented). If there is one, there's also the chance it's already ruled by another supernatural being playing god who may be older and/or stronger than you. Assuming that I've understood MtA correctly, vampires must've found the Awakened insufferable without the consensus to tell them that they can't cast fireball or turn night to day like flicking a light switch or something.<br /> <br /> If there isn't a premade one, they'd need to learn that their disciplines exist and what they do (I imagine it's instinctive) discover what vitae can do when you feed it to things (not so sure that's instinctive, generally vampire instincts are geared towards eating other people's blood) and make their own city.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 05-16-2021, 08:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T18:11:51-07:00","id":1448735,"title":"","content":"While others have the whole history side nailed down, let me bring up the nature of vampirism.<br /> <br /> Once upon a time the 13th generation were thinblooded and couldn\u2019t embrace; but over time thickened such that 14th, 15th and even 16th generations are a thing. And there is also evidence of this having happened before... at one point even the 4th Gen were thinblooded.<br /> <br /> But you need to decide what that means. In pre-history does 4th Generation actually mean 50 blood points and maximum traits of 9 (or Blood Potency 10 in V5) or did that level of potency only come after millennia of thickening blood?<br /> <br /> Your decision is going to matter alot for your campaign. Let\u2019s use V20 since it\u2019s what I\u2019m more familiar with, but if the blood was every bit as potent except for embracing then players will build with that in mind. Attribute and Ability ratings of 6+ and blood pools of 30+ (because I can\u2019t recall any vampires of 6th or higher prior to the bronze age) will be a thing.<br /> <br /> Conversely, if the blood thickens in all ways then the Antediluvians of 3000 BC might only have the stats and limits of a 7th Gen in the modern nights and, stretching time periods a bit the 8th Gen would have the limits of a 13th Gen in the modern nights. The rank 7+ disciplines are still future developments at this early stage and diablerie of the antediluvians by their childer not yet something that could only happen by virtual fiat.<br /> <br /> Worth noting in that regard too is one of the footnotes in The Book of Nod was the actual description of the number of antediluvians is traditionally read as \u201cthree and ten\u201d (13), but might also be read as \u201c3 by 10\u201d (30) and this early in history the antediluvians might not yet have lost the ability to die by the hand of non-antediluvians.<br /> <br /> Similarly, using V5, it could be that all Blood Potencies start at 1 and higher than 5 (or 6 or wherever you set your limit) just aren\u2019t possible in these early nights. By contrast starting the blood potencies where they fall in the Modern Nights means that most vampires can\u2019t gain sustenance from animals at all and will be virtually ravenous unless they murder humans regularly.<br /> <br /> But I\u2019m only raising the questions; it\u2019s up to you to decide the best answers for your campaign.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T21:53:06-07:00","id":1448751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rhywbeth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448685-how-would-you-handle-a-story-set-in-human-and-kindred-prehistory?p=1448727#post1448727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think I had Caine's &quot;cursed to wander the earth alone&quot; thing at the forefront of my brain (was that part of the curse, or am I imagining things?)<br /> <br /> Taking over a city is the goal for a vampire who wants to survive, provided that there is a city around to rule (and that they've been invented). If there is one, there's also the chance it's already ruled by another supernatural being playing god who may be older and/or stronger than you. Assuming that I've understood MtA correctly, vampires must've found the Awakened insufferable without the consensus to tell them that they can't cast fireball or turn night to day like flicking a light switch or something.<br /> <br /> If there isn't a premade one, they'd need to learn that their disciplines exist and what they do (I imagine it's instinctive) discover what vitae can do when you feed it to things (not so sure that's instinctive, generally vampire instincts are geared towards eating other people's blood) and make their own city.</div> </div> </div> </div>Caines isolation is a subjective concept, he is forever removed from the mortal coil and from human life, but obviously he was able to sire more kindred so being alone was easily countered.<br /> <br /> The ancient mages were of a magnitude greater in power than those of the modern nights on top of not being constrained by paradox, so it is more a question of a mage with the right spheres just turning the vampire back into a human, rather than tossing a paradox free fireball. I have a vague memory of there being a small splat book for mage that discussed ancient magics and arch mages. Much like Exalted, cast spell to affect continent grade magic, rather than magic missile without issues. <br /> <br /> In some ways the Prehistory setting is a bit of trap. It lures in the players with promises of incredible stats &amp; abilities and a total lack of masquerade restrictions, only to have the PCs run face first into an almost Dark Souls level of difficulty to survive the other supernaturals. Bunkering down in a city with your own clan or building a new city where no other supernatural has a claim seems the only survival strategy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-18T16:23:58-07:00","id":1448911,"title":"","content":"Vampires before Cainites was one of Saulot's secrets he unlocked. He went to Asia and found out about the Wheel of Ages, that Caine knew about it, and tried to break the cycle by becoming God himself (this happened in Transylvania Chronicle IV). Saulot found out that Caine was just another vampire but just outside the cycle. I did a rough timeline of that era and some conclusions were very interesting.<br /> <br /> Kueijin came first, then Caine and the curse, then the First City and afterwards, the flood. Establishing a historical flood can be fictionalized by the many accounts of the cultural and pseudo-science as it was explained above in another post around 10300 BC to 9500 BC due to all the myths and archaeological records. Now here is when civilization apparently originated in the Anatolian plateau according mostly to the last decades findings. I'd include the period of the Second City around the beginning of the holocene as humans had their agricultural revolution. It wasn't until Saulot left the Second City towards Asia for the first time that he learned from Xue and adapted Chi'iu Muh to Valeren. Therefore, which were Salubri clan disciplines before this time?<br /> <br /> Survivors of the Second City can be the precursor of every myth during prehistory through oral tradition. Tbh I don't involve other games to have an opinion on how they could affect vampires around this time.<br /> <br /> - Saga","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1594-saga","name":"Saga","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-19T00:06:14-07:00","id":1448945,"title":"","content":"I say go the Ancient Aliens Route. Random Space gods that are as much the parents of magical traditions as technology who go around doing random shit like making vampires, and teaching people to build pyramids. a High fantasy magitech age where everyone is wrong(except one Etherite).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-27T16:23:45-07:00","id":1449886,"title":"","content":"Piggybacking onto Black Fox's excellent post, reframing the Deluge as potentially any mistranslated and misunderstood catastrophe severe enough to force humanity to reset opens up the possibility that it happened in the Ice Age.<br /> <br /> The Last Glacial Period includes the period in which humanity is believed to have migrated around the world from its point of origin, and is believed today to have started around 115,000 years ago and ended around 11,700 years ago. <br /> <br /> Screw 40 days and 40 nights! A hundred thousand years of competing for blood on glaciers and tundra? Now THAT's an experience where undead kings and queens go in and unhinged Gehenna monsters come out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11599,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","user":"Reasor","date":". 05-27-2021, 08:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-27T19:29:54-07:00","id":1449906,"title":"","content":"I've frequently pondered the idea that anything before @2500BC or so in the WoD doesn't really conform to our real world history, instead being multiple alternate realities - The First City, the Dragon Kings, Meru, Impergium, etc. -all crashing together in an event vaguely remembered as the Flood or the Sundering. So now there's all this debris left over for various mystery archaeologists to uncover and puzzle over.<br /> <br /> In my head, I kind of imagine one of those being a dark fantasy sword and sorcery type thing (more akin to Conan than Exalted), with larger than life vampire characters striding across a torchlit world of mad cults, powerful blood magic, and dark gods.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}